NIST XCOM photon cross-section data
-----------------------------------
The attenuation tables embedded in every lmparticle binary (data/xcom.csv) are values of the NIST XCOM
database for the elements H, C, N, O, Na, Mg, Al, Si, P, S, Ar, K, Ca, Fe, Cu, Ge, I, W and Pb
(coherent, incoherent, photoelectric and pair-production coefficients, 1 keV to 20 MeV), retrieved
from https://physics.nist.gov/PhysRefData/Xcom/html/xcom1.html with scripts/fetch-xcom.py.
Compounds and mixtures are formed from them by the mixture rule.

  M.J. Berger, J.H. Hubbell, S.M. Seltzer, J. Chang, J.S. Coursey, R. Sukumar, D.S. Zucker, and K. Olsen,
  XCOM: Photon Cross Sections Database, NIST Standard Reference Database 8 (XGAM), NBSIR 87-3597,
  National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, MD (1998). DOI: 10.18434/T48G6X

NIST data are produced by employees of the United States Government and are not subject to copyright in
the United States (17 U.S.C. 105); NIST asks that the database be cited, which is done here. The data
are provided "as is" without warranty. Material compositions follow the NIST "Composition of Materials"
tables (X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients, Hubbell and Seltzer, NISTIR 5632).

Physics models
--------------
Klein-Nishina cross section and sampling (Klein and Nishina 1929; the sampling decomposition is that of the
EGS4 and Geant4 codes, re-implemented here from the published algorithm), xoshiro256** random numbers
(Blackman and Vigna, public domain) and splitmix64 seeding are implemented from their published
descriptions; no code of EGS, Geant4 or MCNP is included.
